# New TestNG tests

Read `src/test/java/com/practicetestautomation/tests/login/LoginTests.java` and the uncommented methods in `src/test/java/com/practicetestautomation/tests/exceptions/ExceptionsTests.java` before adding a test. `BaseTest` already starts and closes the browser.

## Class

- Put the class in `src/test/java/com/practicetestautomation/tests/<area>/`. Login scenarios stay in `com.practicetestautomation.tests.login.LoginTests`. Exceptions scenarios stay in `com.practicetestautomation.tests.exceptions.ExceptionsTests`. A new practice page gets its own class in a new subpackage.
- Extend `BaseTest`. Use the inherited `driver` and `logger`. Do not construct a `WebDriver`, call `driver.quit()`, or set browser options. `BaseTest.setup` reads the suite parameter `browser` (`chrome`, `edge`, or `firefox`). Any other value, including `safari`, starts Chrome.
- One public `@Test` method per scenario. Name it in camelCase and end it with `Test`.
- Import `org.testng.Assert` and `org.testng.annotations.*`. Do not import `By`, `WebDriverWait`, or `ExpectedConditions`.

## Method body

- Construct the page with the inherited driver, then open it: `new LoginPage(driver)` followed by `visit()`.
- Call page-object methods only. Inputs and expected strings stay in the test or in suite parameters. Locators and waits stay on the page object.
- Assert in the test with `org.testng.Assert`. Use `assertEquals(actual, expected)` for text and URLs. Use `assertTrue(condition, "what failed")` for display checks, as `ExceptionsTests` does.
- Do not leave commented-out Selenium. The comment blocks in `ExceptionsTests` are the old raw-driver steps and are not a template.
- Each new test asserts a different outcome. `noSuchElementExceptionsTest` and `timeOutExceptionTest` currently perform the same steps; do not add another copy.
- A positive login test uses groups `{"positive","regression","smoke"}` and the same `executeLogin` credentials already used in `loginFunctionalityTest`.
- A negative login test uses `@Parameters({"username","password","expectedErrorMessage"})` and groups `{"negative","regression"}`. Do not hardcode those three values in the method.
- `ExceptionsTests` methods have no groups. Do not add groups there unless a suite filters on them.
- Use `logger.info` only for a short line about what the test is verifying, as `LoginTests` does. Do not create a new `Logger`.

## Suite XML

`mvn test` runs `src/test/resources/TestSuites/${suiteXmlFile}` only. The default file is `FullRegressionSuite.xml`. A new method does not run until a suite includes it.

- Suites use `<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">` and `verbose="1"`.
- If the suite lists `<methods><include name="..."/>`, add an `<include>` for the new method. `FullRegressionSuite.xml` does this for `loginFunctionalityTest` and `negativeLoginTest`. `DebugSuite.xml` does this for each exceptions method.
- If the suite lists the class and no `<methods>` filter, every `@Test` on that class runs. `ExceptionsSuite.xml` and the exceptions `<test>` in `FullRegressionSuite.xml` are like that.
- `SmokeTestSuite.xml` runs `LoginTests` methods in the `smoke` group and does not set `browser`, so Chrome is used. A new smoke test needs group `smoke` on a class that suite includes.
- Set `browser` to `chrome`, `firefox`, or `edge`. Do not add another `safari` value.
- A `<test>` that includes `negativeLoginTest` must supply `username`, `password`, and `expectedErrorMessage`. Copy the `<parameter>` shape from `FullRegressionSuite.xml`.
